course: dictionary
lesson: 15075
title: Hidden meanings in business words
level: primary-6
stars: 0
explain: Some words sound nicer than what they really mean. A word that does this is called a euphemism. Business people use a lot of them, and rightsizing is one. It sounds sensible and tidy, but in business it often means cutting jobs to make a company smaller. «The company is rightsizing, and 200 people have lost their jobs.» The clue is the people losing their jobs, so rightsizing must mean cutting jobs here.
explain: Rightsizing has a second, plain meaning: making something the right size for what it is used for. «We are rightsizing our computer system, so it runs faster and fits our needs.» Nobody loses a job there. The sentence is about a computer, not people. Always check the words around the word to see which meaning fits.
explain: To test a meaning, swap it into the sentence. If you put «cutting jobs» in place of rightsizing in the first example, it still makes sense, so you have the right meaning. If you tried it in the computer sentence, it would sound silly, so the second meaning fits there.
fact: Rightsizing is a noun (uncountable) and also the -ing form of the verb rightsize: rightsize, rightsizes, rightsized, rightsizing.
fact: «Rightsizing» means cutting jobs to make a company smaller, said in a gentle way. It is business jargon, which means special words used in work.
fact: A euphemism is a softer, politer word used in place of a harsh one. Rightsizing is a euphemism for cutting jobs.
fact: «Rightsize» can also mean making anything the right size for its use, such as a house or a computer system.
fact: «Downsizing» means much the same as rightsizing, but it sounds harsher. Rightsizing is softer still.
exercise: «The company is rightsizing, and 200 people have lost their jobs.» What does rightsizing mean here? | Making the office bigger for more people | Moving to a bigger building by the sea | *Cutting jobs to make the company smaller | Painting the walls a new colour
exercise: «We are rightsizing our computer system, so it runs faster and fits our needs.» What does rightsizing mean here? | Choosing a new holiday | *Making the system the right size for its use | Turning the computers off for ever | Firing the computer team
exercise: Ruby says rightsizing always means that people will lose their jobs. Is she right? | No, because it means the same as happy | Yes, because it is a long word | Yes, it always means that | *No, it can also mean making something the right size for its use
test:
«The manager said the team was rightsizing, but everyone knew some people would be let go.» Why might the manager pick the word rightsizing? | *It sounds gentler and more sensible than cutting jobs | It means exactly the same as a holiday | It is a more polite way to say hello | It means the team will get bigger
test:
«The school is rightsizing the library and moving the books into a smaller room.» What is rightsizing used for here? | *Making the library the right size for its use | Cutting the number of teachers | Closing the school for good | Making the books louder
exam:
«Rightsizing» is a euphemism. What is a euphemism? | *A softer, politer word used in place of a harsher one | A word that means the opposite of another word | A long word with many letters | A word that is always spelled wrong
